          <Pad Id="58GNEO2ft946ks6G9PZ4JN" Bounds="2771,3061,401,535" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="If all of this at this point seems to be an overkill for you to grasp mentally, it is save to say that you could leave this theoretical terrain for now. It is not everybody‘s piece of cake to dive into these concepts and one might also just be interested to simply use the nodes that are available from the start. &#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;However, this is the way, all of the nodes used in this tutorial series are working in the background. All of them have parts that are running during the runtime of vvvv on Update, something that is running once when initialized on Create and an infinite number of parts that are running on specific operations - each operation represented by an individual color.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;It is good that you have been at least confronted once with these terms. Simply knowing about principles like these, which are more in the realm of programming VL than using vvvv will heavily increase your degree of freedom in expressing what you want to achieve. Also, when you are struggling with something and ask in the chat or forum, you will most likely understand the answer that you are getting from the community. This will save time both for you and the person, who helps you: Win-Win.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Like mentioned in the last chapter, there are more types of nodes that you can define, most important the Record and the Class. But for the rest of this tutorial they are not that important. Nonetheless - if you want to go deeper into the topic of object-oriented programming in VL and get started with defining your own custom Records and Classes, I want to point you to this workshop from Node20: ">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="78HL8juflxzrkA8aIU0BHE" Bounds="2772,3619,215,19" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="https://vimeo.com/467436333">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Link</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="4xSmjaJlFWhdrlPFymu0LT" Bounds="2771,2427,401,112" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Here is another example of a Process Node in which multiple operations exist to move and scale a three-dimensional object. This time we also make use of toggles to enable or disable the Grow and Shrink operations, just to show that they actually only evaluate while the input is true. If we use a Bang for this, we just set it to true for one frame.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
