          <Overlay Id="2TsBiDmGA3wp8LJTQ7u0qF" Name="" Bounds="807,3870,4576,80">
            <p:ColorIndex p:Type="Int32">4</p:ColorIndex>
          </Overlay>
          <Pad Id="120UzHTBnFktCHlACepXuw" Bounds="804,4184,401,459" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Now that we know how to define a Record and give it custom operations, let's look at the first real reason you would reach for one. The case is simple but important - bundling related properties together so they travel as one thing through your patch.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;In this chapter we will build the same small application twice. First the way you might build it with what you already knew before chapter 38 - using parallel spreads, one for each property. Then we will rebuild it with a single spread of Records. The patches do exactly the same thing, but the second version is dramatically simpler to read, modify, and extend.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="2b9eTFhIEPGtl1DvxyVbAe" Bounds="1272,4198,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The Old Way: Parallel Spreads">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="4ODjcupkPL4IR0R3gDKaAa" Bounds="1272,4240,401,330" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Imagine we want to draw a field of circles. Each circle has its own position, its own size, and its own color. There are twenty of them, arranged in a spread.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Without a Record, you would do this with three separate spreads - one for positions, one for sizes, one for colors. Each spread has twenty values. You would generate these spreads with the techniques from chapter 21, then iterate over all three at once with ForEach.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The patch on the right shows this approach. Notice how three separate spreads have to be generated, fed in parallel into a ForEach region, and then drawn together. The Position, Size, and Color of each circle live in three completely different parts of the patch, even though they belong together.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="0w76xZMp59zu0EoblG2a2Z" Bounds="1272,4565,401,228" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="**Live element (x=92, y=720, width ~1400):**&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;A wide horizontal patch showing:&#xD;&#xA;- Three Spread Generator chains: one producing a `Spread&lt;Vector2&gt;` (positions, arranged in a grid via Range and CartesianGrid or similar), one producing a `Spread&lt;Float&gt;` (sizes, varied via random or Sin), one producing a `Spread&lt;Color&gt;` (colors, varied via HSV across a Range).&#xD;&#xA;- All three feeding into a single ForEach region.&#xD;&#xA;- Inside the ForEach: a Circle drawn at the position, with the size and color applied.&#xD;&#xA;- Output on a SkiaRenderer showing twenty circles.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The pedagogical point is *visual congestion* - three parallel data paths converging into one ForEach, with the relationship between the three values held together only by the implicit alignment of spread indices.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">7</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="5b6q8kI7pOCbiGel9RqmnX" Bounds="1761,4176,371,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The New Way: A Spread of Records">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="3eW3ETi52fQXs7ngixTQQ6" Bounds="1761,4218,401,330" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Now let's rebuild the same field using a single Record that bundles Position, Size, and Color together. We define a Record called Spot with these three properties. Then we generate a Spread&lt;Spot&gt; with twenty Spots in it. The ForEach iterates over the spread, and inside it we use Split to read each Spot's three properties at once.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The result on screen is identical. But look at the patch. We have one data stream instead of three. The ForEach takes one input. The relationship between Position, Size, and Color is now structural - they live inside the same Record, so the patch literally cannot mix them up. If you ever need to add a fourth property, like Rotation, you add it to the Record once and every Spot has it.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;This is what bundling buys you. Related data stays related, in the patch as much as in your head.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="5mo6G5k6yFT9eZyRNioIbq" Bounds="1761,4543,401,292" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="**Live element (x=92, y=1220, width ~1400):**&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;A wide horizontal patch showing:&#xD;&#xA;- A `Spot` Record definition (Position: Vector2, Size: Float, Color: Color) - sitting in the Patch Explorer, referenced here.&#xD;&#xA;- A spread generator that produces a `Spread&lt;Spot&gt;` - likely using the same underlying spreads from the previous patch (Range for positions, etc.) but combined with Create Spot into a single Spread&lt;Spot&gt; via ForEach or SelectMany.&#xD;&#xA;- A single ForEach region taking the Spread&lt;Spot&gt;.&#xD;&#xA;- Inside: Split Spot, feeding Position, Size, Color into a Circle drawing operation.&#xD;&#xA;- The same SkiaRenderer showing twenty circles - identical visual output to the previous patch.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**Pedagogical note:** The visual comparison is the entire argument. The new patch is noticeably less wide and has fewer crossing lines than the old one. That visual simplification is what the reader should feel.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">7</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="4xI5Mlmu0ijJFfiuIR5HGJ" Bounds="2246,4157,371,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Why This Is Better">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="3a8fwEZZEvEyl0adMkUb04" Bounds="2246,4199,399,371" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The bundled version wins in three ways the reader can feel directly.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;First, the patch is smaller. One data stream instead of three. Fewer links crossing the canvas, fewer parallel structures to keep aligned in your head.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Second, the structure mirrors the meaning. A Spot is one thing, and the patch treats it as one thing. When you read the patch, you do not have to mentally reassemble three parallel spreads into the concept of a &quot;field of spots.&quot; The Record does that work for you.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Third, the patch is easier to change. If you want every Spot to also have a Rotation, you add Rotation to the Record once. Every existing patch that uses Spots immediately has access to it. With the parallel-spread version, adding a Rotation means adding a fourth spread, threading it into the ForEach, and making sure its length matches the other three.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="71LkOwtNyKqoXwcZBKA2It" Bounds="2692,4176,371,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="When to Bundle">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="3p1LYpLMxH1ZINXhPjJWoB" Bounds="2692,4218,399,405" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The honest answer is - almost always, if the values belong together.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;A good Record bundles properties that conceptually describe one thing. A Spot has a Position, Size, and Color because each Spot has its own. A character in a game has Health, Position, and Inventory because each character has its own. A particle has its own Position, Velocity, and Radius.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The test is simple. If you find yourself maintaining several parallel spreads where the values at each index belong to the same conceptual thing, those values want to be a Record. Bundle them and the parallel structure disappears.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The opposite case - where you should not bundle - is when the values do not belong to one thing. The current time, the mouse position, and the application's frame count are all values, but they do not describe one entity. They describe the global state of the world. Bundling them into a Record would be artificial and would make the patch harder to read, not easier.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Overlay Id="33phItzaDPOJZIFbOvT8Pb" Bounds="3366,4236,383,298">
            <p:ColorIndex p:Type="Int32">10</p:ColorIndex>
          </Overlay>
          <Pad Id="0KAY3QRBqiCrChuJyZZDsj" Bounds="3367,4258,216,25" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Other Resources">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">12</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="7gFETPEfkO54BlUKeo2O8W" Bounds="3367,4289,197,19" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The Gray Book on Records">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="5Gd4aaGXYd1Po63wOAXV1o" Bounds="3367,4308,360,54" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="https://thegraybook.vvvv.org/reference/language/types.html">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Link</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="1sC5RwmVJOpeoT7PpZoD5e" Bounds="92,4329,540,908" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="**1. The Spot, not the Particle.** This chapter uses `Spot` as its small dry Record. The Particle is held back for chapter 41 where it earns its place as the carrying example through Part V's later chapters. Spot is deliberately less evocative than Particle - a flat, static, organizational case. This signals to the reader that chapter 40 is about *structure*, not *behavior*.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**2. The side-by-side construction.** The chapter's whole argument is comparative. Two identical applications, built two ways, with the reader feeling the difference. No abstract claims about clean code or maintainability - just the patches themselves. The reader who has just read this chapter will look at their next patch and ask &quot;could this be a Record?&quot; That's the chapter's job.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**3. No new mechanics introduced.** Everything used here is from chapters 38, 39, and earlier. The chapter is purely applied - it teaches the reader to *reach for* a Record, not how to define one. This matches the precedent of chapter 28 (Collision), which is purely applied use of chapter 27 (Mouse Input).&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**4. The &quot;When to Bundle&quot; column is short.** A brief, honest test for when bundling is the right move. Avoids the trap of providing a comprehensive design framework - the reader at this point benefits more from a quick gut check than from formal criteria.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**5. Forward reference to chapter 41 is implicit.** The chapter does not say &quot;next we will look at the case where Records come alive.&quot; It just ends. Chapter 41 picks up the next case on its own terms. The reader knows from the chapter list that Use Case II is coming.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**6. No back-references in the prose.** The chapter is short and self-contained. Earlier chapters are mentioned only in the resources panel and via implicit reliance (ForEach from chapter 20, Spread Generators from chapter 21). The chapter feels like it stands on its own foundation.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**7. Length.** Roughly four columns of body text, two substantial side-by-side live patches. Comparable in scope to chapter 28 (Collision) and chapter 30 (Counting) - applied chapters that don't need to introduce much new vocabulary.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="3IDLAIiBcBM2W2sBtHUlwt" Bounds="92,4106,515,197" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The first applied chapter. The organizational case for Records. Take a problem the reader could solve with parallel spreads of separate values, then show how a single spread of Records makes the same patch dramatically cleaner. The reader feels the wiring shrink, ForEach loops simplify, and related data stays together.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;This chapter introduces no new mechanics - everything used here was covered in chapters 38 and 39 (defining Records, custom operations) and earlier parts of the series (Spreads, ForEach, Iteration). Its job is purely to demonstrate *why bundling pays off*.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
